📦 Python Collections

What are Collections?

**Collections** in Python are built-in data structures used to store and manage multiple values efficiently.
They allow grouping of related data and provide different behaviors based on **order, mutability, and uniqueness**.

Python’s core collections are:

* List
* Tuple
* Set
* Dictionary

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📋 List (Collection)

Definition

A **list** is an ordered, mutable collection that can store elements of different data types.

Characteristics

* Ordered
* Mutable
* Allows duplicate values
* Supports indexing & slicing
* Dynamic in size

Use Cases

* Storing changeable data
* Iterative processing
* Data manipulation tasks

Limitations

* Slower lookups compared to sets/dictionaries
* Uses more memory than tuples

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📦 Tuple (Collection)

Definition

A **tuple** is an ordered but immutable collection of elements.

Characteristics

* Ordered
* Immutable
* Allows duplicates
* Faster than lists
* Hashable (can be dictionary keys)

Use Cases

* Fixed data records
* Data protection
* Function return values

Limitations

* Cannot modify elements

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧺 Set (Collection)

Definition

A **set** is an unordered collection of unique elements.

Characteristics

* Unordered
* No duplicates
* Mutable
* No indexing
* Fast membership checking

Use Cases

* Removing duplicates
* Membership tests
* Mathematical operations

Limitations

* No indexing or slicing
* Order not preserved

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🗂 Dictionary (Collection)

Definition

A **dictionary** stores data in **key-value pairs**, where keys map to values.

Characteristics

* Keys must be unique & immutable
* Values can be any data type
* Mutable
* Very fast lookups (O(1))
* Maintains insertion order (Python 3.7+)

Use Cases

* Mapping relationships
* JSON/API data
* Configuration data


🔍 Collection Comparison (Concept)

| Feature      | List | Tuple | Set | Dictionary |
| ------------ | ---- | ----- | --- | ---------- |
| Ordered      | ✔️   | ✔️    | ❌   | ✔️         |
| Mutable      | ✔️   | ❌     | ✔️  | ✔️         |
| Duplicates   | ✔️   | ✔️    | ❌   | Keys ❌     |
| Indexing     | ✔️   | ✔️    | ❌   | Keys       |
| Lookup Speed | ❌    | ❌     | ✔️  | ✔️         |


⚡ Python Comprehensions

What are Comprehensions?

**Comprehensions** provide a concise and readable way to create collections using expressions instead of loops.

They follow a **functional programming style**.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📋 List Comprehension

Concept

Creates a list by applying an expression to each item in an iterable.

Syntax

```python
[expression for item in iterable if condition]
```

Advantages

* Cleaner code
* Faster execution
* More Pythonic

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧺 Set Comprehension

Concept

Creates a set using comprehension syntax.

Characteristics

* Removes duplicates automatically
* Unordered output
* Uses `{}` brackets

Syntax

```python
{expression for item in iterable if condition}
```

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🗂 Dictionary Comprehension

Concept

Creates dictionaries dynamically using expressions.

Syntax

```python
{key_expression: value_expression for item in iterable if condition}
```

Advantages

* Efficient transformations
* Cleaner mapping logic

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📦 Generator Expression

Concept

A **generator expression** creates an iterator instead of storing all values at once.

### Characteristics

* Uses `()` parentheses
* Lazy evaluation
* Memory efficient

When to Use

* Large datasets
* Streaming data
* Performance-critical applications

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔁 Comprehension vs Traditional Loop

| Aspect         | Loop   | Comprehension |
| -------------- | ------ | ------------- |
| Code Length    | More   | Less          |
| Readability    | Medium | High          |
| Performance    | Slower | Faster        |
| Memory Usage   | Higher | Optimized     |
| Pythonic Style | ❌     |✔️            |

